How the Worker Travels in the Plant
How the Worker Finds the Shortest Route
The Travel Mode determines how the Worker finds the shortest route from the WorkerPool and the
Workplaces and between the Workplaces.
Remarks
You can select to make the Worker:
• Move freely within area
• Walk along footpaths
• Beam to workplace
See also
How the Worker Decides Where to Work
How the Worker Travels in the Plant
11-3144 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Move freely within area
Makes the Worker walk freely within the area of the simulation model and walk around the obstacles,
which you defined.
Remarks
Plant Simulation automatically computes the shortest route between the WorkPlaces and between the
WorkerPool and the WorkPlaces. It uses the layout of the model to compute the shortest route to a
WorkPlace.
Note
We use the 3D layout to compute the route of the Worker. It normally represents the looks of your facility
close to reality, meaning that the position, the dimensions, and the orientation of the machines and the
fixtures of the building closely match the real plant.
To compute the shortest route, Plant Simulation uses information about those areas of the model, which
the Worker cannot walk on or cross. These areas encompass all kinds of obstacles, for example walls,
pillars, machines, conveyors, safety fences, etc. Plant Simulation automatically detects these areas. By
default, we marked the material flow objects, the animatable objects, and the graphics, which belong to
the Frame, as Barred Areas. Information flow objects and user interface objects by default are not Barred
Areas. You can set if an object as an obstacle for the Worker under Edit 3D Properties > Tab Graphics >
Obstacle for the Worker. For graphics, you can do this under Edit 3D Properties > Tab Graphic Settings,
for example to allow the Worker to cross a Track. MUs do not provide this setting.
All settings, except for None automatically create a barred area.
To designate an area in the plant, which the Worker can enter, but should not, for example the pivoting
area of a crane, you can manually create a Barred Area.
To allow the Worker to walk around or through obstacles, use a FootPath. Let's say that you want your
Worker to walk through a door which in part of a wall that you defined as an obstacle. Then you can lay a
FootPath through the door on which he can walk through it. The FootPath always has to be a little longer
than the depth of the wall.
Move freely within area
Plant Simulation Help 11-3145
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

For the FootPath not the length, which you type in on the tab Attributes is decisive, but the Length of the
FootPath in the scene. The distance covered of the Worker is determined by the starting point and the end
point of the FootPath as well by the anchor points.
If the FootPath includes an incline, the Worker moves slower on the incline than on a flat surface. Plant
Simulation achieves this by extending the imagined distance for the incline segments. When computing
the length of an inclined segment, Plant Simulation triples the height difference.
Compare this example: A segment of a FootPath has a dimension of 1 m in the x-direction and 1 m in the
z-direction, thus actually is 1.414 m long. The imagined dimension however is 1 m in the x-direction and 3
m in z-direction, so that Plant Simulation computes an effective length of 3.162 m.
The Worker always walks on the route, which has the shortest effective length and which he can thus
cover in the shortest time. This can cause the Worker to take an actually longer but flatter route to the
destination instead of a shorter route which contains inclines and gradients.
For computing the effective route length Plant Simulation triples the height difference. The resulting
speeds of the Worker are realistic in general. The speed of the Worker decreases to a third, when the
Worker moves vertically up or down, for example when climbing a ladder. The speed decreases to about
half, when the Worker negotiates an incline of 38 degrees, for example a when walking on a stairway.
Note
The Worker walks slower on an incline than he walks on a flat surface.
Note
When the Worker walks freely within the area, he can only get to a different floor in the building by
walking up or down a ramp on a Footpath or walking up and down Stairs.
The example model Factory 51 demonstrates how you can use Stairways.
Move freely within area
11-3146 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

