How the Worker Decides Where to Work
At times a Worker in the plant will have to decide if he continues working on a Workplace doing a job for
which he has the correct skills or if he moves to another Workplace that requires his skills more urgently.
Remarks
While brokering, the Broker and the Worker proceed as follows:
1. The Broker first checks the Priority of the current request in the dialog of the Worker.
If the job has the highest priority, he stays on the current Workplace and finishes the current job.
2. The Worker then checks if the material flow object, to which the Workplace is assigned, has a Scope
with the respective Objects in his dialog.
How the Worker Decides Where to Work
11-3152 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If the current material flow object is part of the correct Scope, he stays on the current Workplace and
finishes his current job there.
3. The respective Broker also checks if Choose the Nearest Worker is selected.
4. If the Worker has the highest Priority, a matching or no Scope, and the shortest route to the
Workplace, the Broker mediates him to the respective Workplace.
In doing so, Plant Simulation computes a route to the Workplace for all eligible Workers while
brokering them. The more Workers can be brokered, the more time it takes to compute the route to
the Workplace. If you configured additional services, this might use up even more time. This can,
consequently, slow down the simulation.
See also
How the Broker Mediates Jobs to the Worker
