How the Worker Carries Parts
The Worker can carry parts from one station to another station. This only works if you attach a Workplace
to the work station at which the Worker picks the part up. Also attach a Workplace to the target station at
which he places the part. You also have to insert a Broker and a WorkerPool.
Remarks
Select the Travel Mode in the WorkerPool:
• For the setting Move freely within area the Worker automatically finds his way to the destination of the
part.
• For the setting Walk along Footpaths you have to connect the Workplaces with Footpaths. Also
connect the WorkerPool and the Workplace at the station at which the Worker picks the part up, with a
Footpath.
Activate the transport importer on the tab Importer > sub-tab Transport the dialog of the material flow
object at which the Worker is to pick the part up. In the example below this is the station named Station1.
How the Worker Carries Parts
11-3154 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Then, specify the Broker who brokers the Worker. If the part itself does not have a destination, you
also have to specify the MU Target to which he carries the part. The MU Target either is the Workplace
attached to the work station, which processes the part next and to which the Worker carries the part, or
the work station itself.
The part then requests a Worker. The parts wait at the station until the Worker arrives and picks them up:
• If the Worker can only carry a single part, if its capacity is 1 (X-Dimension = 1, Y-Dimension = 1,
Z-Dimension = 1), he picks the part up and carries it to the destination.
• If the Worker can carry several parts, he will wait for parts until his capacity is reached. To prevent the
Worker from waiting too long for additional parts to arrive, type in the Maximum Dwell Time. After it
has passed, he moves to the target station, the MU Target, and places the part.
The Worker evaluates the MU Targets and moves to the closest one first. At the target station, he places
all parts destined for this station. If he carries additional parts, he evaluates the next target and moves
on to the closest one, until he has placed all parts.
If your plant works in shifts, the Worker walks back to his WorkerPool at the end of his shift. He then
deposits the parts which he could not deliver into the Parts Buffer which you assigned to the WorkerPool.
If you did not assign a parts buffer to the WorkerPool, Plant Simulation shows a warning and stops the
simulation.
If you activated the transport importer in the parts buffer, one of the available Workers carries the parts
in the parts buffer to the destination object after the next shift has started. If you did not activate the
How the Worker Carries Parts
Plant Simulation Help 11-3155
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

transport importer, the parts in the parts buffer are moved on according to the Exit Strategy which you
selected for the parts buffer.
See also
Model a Worker Who Carries Parts Between Workplaces
